# tools/kit-boundary.allow — where the app may touch the GPU or a numerics framework (decision D15).
#
# One line per file: <path><TAB><modules the file may import, comma-separated; `-` for a .metal file><TAB><reason>.
# tools/check-kit-boundary.sh fails on a gated import (Accelerate, vecLib, Metal, MetalKit, MetalPerformanceShaders*,
# BNNS, simd) in a file or module not listed here, on any vDSP / cblas / vImage / LAPACK / vForce / BNNS / MPS symbol
# anywhere, and on a .metal file not listed. Numerics belong in the kits; a new line here needs a reason that is display,
# not science, or a lineage/marks record (app-side provenance).

# The display pipeline is MacEMUI's (its Colormaps.metal); the app holds no .metal file. The test of its export twin:
macTEMTests/ImageExportGPUTests.swift	Metal	GPU image export vs CPU rendering
macTEMTests/RoomMemoryTests.swift	Metal	the Metal device's allocated bytes, to name a texture held after a close (#713)

# SEM: the float32 GPU paths of two numeric ports in TEMCore.
Sources/TEMCore/SEM/MasterPattern/MasterPatternProjectionMetal.swift	Metal	SEM master pattern projection: the float32 GPU path of a numeric port that lives in TEMCore because the SEM program changes no kit; checked against the float64 CPU path
Sources/TEMCore/SEM/Indexing/DictionaryIndexingMetal.swift	Metal,MetalPerformanceShaders,+symbols	SEM dictionary indexing: the float32 GPU matrix product (MPSMatrixMultiplication, #624) of a numeric port that lives in TEMCore because the SEM program changes no kit; checked against the float64 CPU path
Sources/TEMCore/SEM/Texture/OrientationDensity.swift	Accelerate,+symbols	SEM ODF: vForce powers in the float64 kernel density of a numeric port that lives in TEMCore because the SEM program changes no kit (#624)
Sources/TEMCore/Diffraction/CrystalPaneModel.swift	simd	the crystal pane hands ShellCrystal its cell and orientation as simd_double3x3: the type alone, no numerics (#790)

# Simulation: the orientation matrix DiffractoKit hands over (simd_double3x3) and its columns; no numerics of the app's own.
Sources/TEMCore/Simulation/SimulationSpecimen.swift	simd	the slab's orientation as DiffractoKit gives it (simd_double3x3), read to name its in-plane angle; the numbers are the kits'
macTEMTests/SimulationSpecimenTests.swift	simd	compares the slab's orientation columns with the pattern's (simd_length)
